# `snprintf` returns the length the formatted output *would* have needed, not the
# number of bytes it actually wrote. When the format expands past the destination
# buffer the call truncates safely but reports the larger figure, so any caller
# that reuses the return value as a byte count or a buffer offset without first
# comparing it against the destination's size steps outside the buffer.
#
# miniupnpc did that at four call sites, each with a fixed-size stack destination
# and a format whose arguments come from the device's own description URL,
# service and action strings:
#
#   miniupnpc/miniwget.c   miniwget3():          len = snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), ...)
#                                                while(sent < len) send(s, buf+sent, len-sent, 0)
#   miniupnpc/minisoap.c   soapPostSubmit():     headerssize = snprintf(headerbuf, sizeof(headerbuf), ...)
#                                                httpWrite(fd, body, bodysize, headerbuf, headerssize)
#   miniupnpc/minissdpc.c  ssdpDiscoverDevices():n = snprintf(bufr, sizeof(bufr), MSearchMsgFmt, ...)
#                                                sendto(sudp, bufr, n, 0, ...)
#   miniupnpc/miniupnpc.c  simpleUPnPcommand2(): soapbodylen = snprintf(soapbody, sizeof(soapbody), ...)
#                                                p = soapbody + soapbodylen; *(p++) = ...
#
# The first three read past the end of the stack buffer and put whatever follows
# it on the wire; the fourth is worse, because the write pointer starts outside
# the buffer and the loop's own margin test (`soapbody + sizeof(soapbody) <= p +
# 100`) compares an out-of-range pointer, so the element writes that
# follow are out-of-bounds writes.
#
# Fix commit 4c90b87ce3d2517097880279e8c3daa7731100e6 ("Check snprintf result")
# adds the same comparison after each of the four calls -- `if ((unsigned int)ret
# >= sizeof(dest)) <bail>` -- and captures the return value in the one place that
# had commented it out (`/*soapbodylen = */snprintf(...)`). The axis is the
# unchecked return value, not a string function without a bound: every one of
# these calls is a bounded `snprintf`. The corpus records CWE-120; the
# behaviour is CWE-252 leading to CWE-125 on three sites and CWE-787 on the
# fourth.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB`
# over the whole repository reports 1371 findings and 435 at or above high at the
# vulnerable revision and 1371 and 435 at the fixed one, stable across three runs
# and two runs respectively, and not
# one of them lands on any of the four sites: the finding lines in those files are
# 376 and 454 either side of miniwget.c:408, 50 and 51 either side of
# minisoap.c:96, and 109 and 146 either side of miniupnpc.c:112-137, all
# `code.NullableDeref`. minissdpc.c does report at :687 and :694 -- inside the
# vulnerable `snprintf` call -- but for `deviceTypes[deviceIndex]` as an
# `code.IndexAccessSink`, which is the format argument and not the defect. The
# vulnerable and patched revisions of miniwget3 scan identically, one finding and
# four flags each, the same `code.NullableDeref`, `code.PointerUse` and two
# `code.IntegerSizeArithmetic` rows, shifted by the inserted lines.
#
# On the fourth site the scanner reports the opposite of the defect. The
# `simpleUPnPcommand2` body below produces a medium/high `VYQL-MEM-016`
# (`code.RawMemoryCopySize`) on the *correctly* bounded `strncpy(p, "...",
# soapbody + sizeof(soapbody) - p)` and nothing on `p = soapbody + soapbodylen`
# or on the `*(p++)` writes that run past the end, which carry only low-confidence
# `code.NullableDeref` rows.
#
# The gap is a missing fact, not a missing binding. Detecting this needs three
# things correlated on one variable inside one function: that it holds a
# formatted-output call's return value, that it is later used as a length
# argument or added to the destination pointer, and that no comparison against
# the destination's size stands between the two. The C frontend's function
# context tokens carry each half separately and name-preserving --
# `assign:len=snprintf`, `call_arg_at:sendto:2:n`, `assign:p=soapbody+soapbodylen`
# -- but binding predicates over them are contains tests with no way to require
# that two tokens name the same variable. The only name-preserving token for the
# comparison is `binary:`, which routes through the file-wide AST path with a
# case-sensitive substring fallback, and the alternative -- matching
# `>=sizeof(` in the function's raw body text -- is both the fixing commit's own
# spelling (`>= (int)sizeof(`, `>= BUFSZ` and `< 0 || >=` are all equally
# correct) and silenceable by a comment, since the C context's body token is the
# source with whitespace stripped and comments intact.
#
# Measured, so a sweep need not re-derive it: the correlated form is worth having.
# A three-part heuristic over source text -- a captured `v?snprintf` return, later
# used as a call length argument or a destination-pointer offset, with no
# comparison of that variable against `sizeof` or the size argument -- selects 5
# functions in `miniupnpc/`, 4 of them these sites and the fifth the same shape in
# `minihttptestserver.c`, and 0 functions across libexpat, libmspack, libpng and
# libredwg. Dropping the "used as a length or offset" half is what makes it
# unshippable: bare "captures the return value with no size comparison" selects
# roughly 70 of the 86 capture sites in this repository.
#
# The pair at the end isolates the construct. The `send(s, buf + sent, len -
# sent, 0)` line is byte-identical in both cases and the only difference is where
# `len` came from: from `recv` it is a labelled source and the size arithmetic
# reports `VYQL-NUM-003`; from `snprintf` it reports nothing, because a formatted
# output call's return value is not a hazardous-length source of any kind. That is
# the whole gap -- the engine's only route to this line is attacker taint, and the
# hazard here is an API contract about a return value that no fact expresses.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1392: the HTTP request length reported by snprintf is sent from a fixed buffer without a truncation check"
  lang c
  file miniupnpc/miniwget.c
  reject VYQL-NUM-003
  reject VYQL-MEM-010
  reject VYQL-MEM-016
  reject_review code.OffsetLengthBoundsOverflow
  reject_review code.UncheckedEncodedLengthArithmetic
  reject_review code.MissingPostCopyBoundsCheck
  reject_review code.PointerOffsetBoundsCheck
  code
  ```
  #include <stdio.h>
  #include <string.h>

  void * miniwget3(const char * host, unsigned short port, const char * path,
                   int * size, const char * httpversion)
  {
  	char buf[2048];
  	int s;
  	int n;
  	int len;
  	int sent;
  	void * content;

  	*size = 0;
  	s = connecthostport(host, port, 0);
  	if(s < 0)
  		return NULL;

  	len = snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf),
  	               "GET %s HTTP/%s\r\n"
  	               "Host: %s:%d\r\n"
  	               "Connection: Close\r\n"
  	               "\r\n",
  	               path, httpversion, host, port);
  	sent = 0;
  	while(sent < len)
  	{
  		n = send(s, buf + sent, len - sent, 0);
  		if(n < 0)
  		{
  			closesocket(s);
  			return NULL;
  		}
  		else
  		{
  			sent += n;
  		}
  	}
  	content = getHTTPResponse(s, size);
  	closesocket(s);
  	return content;
  }
  ```

test "rank1392: the same request abandons the send once snprintf reports the buffer is too small"
  lang c
  file miniupnpc/miniwget.c
  reject VYQL-NUM-003
  reject VYQL-MEM-010
  reject VYQL-MEM-016
  reject_review code.OffsetLengthBoundsOverflow
  reject_review code.UncheckedEncodedLengthArithmetic
  reject_review code.MissingPostCopyBoundsCheck
  reject_review code.PointerOffsetBoundsCheck
  code
  ```
  #include <stdio.h>
  #include <string.h>

  void * miniwget3(const char * host, unsigned short port, const char * path,
                   int * size, const char * httpversion)
  {
  	char buf[2048];
  	int s;
  	int n;
  	int len;
  	int sent;
  	void * content;

  	*size = 0;
  	s = connecthostport(host, port, 0);
  	if(s < 0)
  		return NULL;

  	len = snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf),
  	               "GET %s HTTP/%s\r\n"
  	               "Host: %s:%d\r\n"
  	               "Connection: Close\r\n"
  	               "\r\n",
  	               path, httpversion, host, port);
  	if ((unsigned int)len >= sizeof(buf))
  	{
  		closesocket(s);
  		return NULL;
  	}
  	sent = 0;
  	while(sent < len)
  	{
  		n = send(s, buf + sent, len - sent, 0);
  		if(n < 0)
  		{
  			closesocket(s);
  			return NULL;
  		}
  		else
  		{
  			sent += n;
  		}
  	}
  	content = getHTTPResponse(s, size);
  	closesocket(s);
  	return content;
  }
  ```

test "rank1392: the SOAP body write pointer starts at the length snprintf reported, so the loop's own margin test compares an out-of-range pointer"
  lang c
  file miniupnpc/miniupnpc.c
  reject VYQL-NUM-003
  reject VYQL-MEM-010
  reject_review code.OffsetLengthBoundsOverflow
  reject_review code.UncheckedEncodedLengthArithmetic
  reject_review code.MissingPostCopyBoundsCheck
  reject_review code.PointerOffsetBoundsCheck
  code
  ```
  #include <stdio.h>
  #include <string.h>

  char * simpleUPnPcommand2(int s, const char * url, const char * service,
                            const char * action, struct UPNParg * args,
                            int * bufsize, const char * httpversion)
  {
  	char soapact[128];
  	char soapbody[2048];
  	char * p;
  	const char * pe, * pv;
  	int soapbodylen;

  	*bufsize = 0;
  	snprintf(soapact, sizeof(soapact), "%s#%s", service, action);
  	soapbodylen = snprintf(soapbody, sizeof(soapbody),
  	                       "<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\r\n"
  	                       "<s:Envelope><s:Body><u:%s xmlns:u=\"%s\">",
  	                       action, service);
  	p = soapbody + soapbodylen;
  	while(args->elt)
  	{
  		if(soapbody + sizeof(soapbody) <= p + 100)
  			return NULL;
  		*(p++) = '<';
  		pe = args->elt;
  		while(*pe)
  			*(p++) = *(pe++);
  		*(p++) = '>';
  		if((pv = args->val))
  		{
  			while(*pv)
  				*(p++) = *(pv++);
  		}
  		args++;
  	}
  	strncpy(p, "></s:Body></s:Envelope>\r\n", soapbody + sizeof(soapbody) - p);
  	return soapbody;
  }
  ```

